WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.679
This is Shaq

00:00:02.679 --> 00:00:04.650
on The Great Wall of China.

00:00:04.650 --> 00:00:06.780
Here’s Michael Jordan,

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.020
There are a lot of these.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:17.280
For years, the NBA has been sending teams to China

00:00:17.290 --> 00:00:21.720
where more people watch 
NBA games than in the United States.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.000
The purpose is to play basketball,

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:25.210
meet Chinese fans,

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.920
and have players like Rip Hamilton
spread a clear message.

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.195
The NBA has worked hard

00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:34.760
to build a successful business in China,

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:39.900
but then they almost 
lost it all because of a single tweet.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:49.680
It was March of 2000 and President Clinton
was pushing for a trade deal with China,

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:52.760
despite concerns about China’s human rights record.

00:00:52.760 --> 00:00:57.280
“It does deny its citizens fundamental rights
of free speech and religious expression.

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.360
It does define its interest in the world in sometimes
 in ways that are dramatically at odds from our own.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.760
Just a decade before, China’s government
put down an uprising at Tiananmen Square.

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:11.240
“...human rights not trade!”

00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:16.740
And in 1999, Americans also had concerns
about China’s record in Taiwan and Tibet.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:22.780
“The Fortune 500 companies are controlling
 the American foreign policy in China.

00:01:22.780 --> 00:01:24.120
This is wrong.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:25.240
It’s gotta stop.

00:01:25.250 --> 00:01:30.040
President Clinton, we are asking you
to take a courageous position.”

00:01:30.040 --> 00:01:31.300
But Clinton insisted that

00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:35.820
doing business with China would have
 a positive impact on their human rights platform.

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:41.000
"The question is not whether we approve or
disapprove of China's practices.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:47.240
The question is: What is the smartest thing
to do to improve these practices?"

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:54.060
Bill Clinton came to think that engagement,
that trade, that direct contact would have

00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:00.900
a positive impact on the human rights situation
in China, as well as improving

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.579
the overall U.S. China relationship.

00:02:03.580 --> 00:02:06.520
The next year, China joined the WTO.

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:12.640
2001 is important because that's when the
country enters into the World Trade Organization,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:19.040
which greatly simplifies doing business, not
just with the United States, but with the entire world.

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:22.480
The Chinese economy was about to take off.

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:25.380
US businesses began investing in China

00:02:25.380 --> 00:02:29.380
and eventually Chinese companies
would start investing back in the US.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:35.520
As China became richer, as China became more
integrated into the global economy,

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.260
it became a more important market
for the United States.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:44.500
After 2001, the average Chinese person had
more disposable income

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:47.700
as a result of the rapid growth.

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:53.100
It was the perfect moment for the NBA to build a business in China and it all started right here.

00:02:53.100 --> 00:03:01.900
“With the first pick in the 2002 NBA Draft, the Houston Rockets select Yao Ming from Shanghai, China!”

00:03:03.620 --> 00:03:09.180
At the time Yao Ming was 21-years old, watching
the NBA draft on a laptop in China.

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:15.140
“Can you fully comprehend, for yourself,
for Chinese Basketball, and for the NBA,

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:18.700
what it means to be the NBA’s number one overall pick?”

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:22.200
Yao was the NBA’s “next big thing” for fans in China,

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:28.100
where basketball has been popular ever since
it was introduced by missionaries in the 19th century.

00:03:28.100 --> 00:03:31.260
When the Communist Party came to power they
banned most Western sports,

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:34.220
but basketball was embraced as a national passion.

00:03:34.900 --> 00:03:37.580
But China never produced a star player...

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:38.300
until Yao.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.960
"...Yao Ming and company, ready to go..."

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:48.590
Now they could watch a Chinese player playing
at the same level of these legendary greats

00:03:48.590 --> 00:03:50.470
and playing Shaq

00:03:50.470 --> 00:03:56.130
200 million Chinese viewers tuned in for his
first game against Shaq and The Lakers.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:04:02.030
“They’re watching live in Shanghai at
10:30 in the morning as O’Neal goes right at Ming!"

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:09.040
Compare that to 9.9 million: the average number
of Americans that watched the NBA Finals that year.

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:12.140
“Yao, first touch, for two!”

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:18.400
Yao became a household name in China and soon
American companies turned to him for endorsements.

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:19.740
“Can Jimmy play?

00:04:19.740 --> 00:04:20.740
Hi Yao!

00:04:20.740 --> 00:04:21.260
Jimmy?”

00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:28.020
“Get a Garmin”

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:29.680
“...the new 17-inch powerbook.”

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:33.620
Yao’s popularity also helped the NBA develop
their fanbase there.

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:40.820
In 2004 The NBA started sending teams on an
annual summer tour of China,

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:45.500
where the Houston Rockets became a fan favorite, simply because that was Yao’s team.

00:04:47.320 --> 00:04:52.720
The tours brought the biggest NBA players
to China and boosted the league’s popularity.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:56.200
For comparison, The NBA 
has slightly more Twitter followers

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:59.040
than the the next 
major sports league in The United States.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:03.660
But in China, the NBA dominates social media
platforms like Weibo.

00:05:03.660 --> 00:05:09.410
Nearly 500 million people watched NBA games
last year using Tencent, China’s largest

00:05:09.410 --> 00:05:10.640
streaming platform.

00:05:10.640 --> 00:05:14.400
That’s more than the entire population of
the United States.

00:05:15.220 --> 00:05:20.680
In 2019 Tencent and The NBA signed
 a deal worth $1.5 billion dollars,

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.820
almost three times what it was worth five years ago.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:30.340
The people who manage the NBA recognized that
there were tremendous opportunities in China

00:05:30.340 --> 00:05:34.940
and they worked hard to develop that market
and they have succeeded.

00:05:35.800 --> 00:05:40.460
Then seven words threatened everything.

00:05:40.470 --> 00:05:43.940
Daryl Morey is the general manager
of the Houston Rockets

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:49.100
and his tweet repeated a phrase
chanted by protesters in Hong Kong.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.280
Well this is just about civil liberties, this
is about having a voice in government.

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:57.280
What's wrong with that?

00:05:57.280 --> 00:06:04.060
And so he thought nothing of tweeting this out, 
whereas in China, it was perceived quite differently.

00:06:04.060 --> 00:06:09.960
It was seen as an effort to break up China,
an effort to weaken China.

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:14.040
The Chinese Consulate in Houston
responded by saying they were “deeply shocked

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:19.520
by the erroneous comments on Hong Kong”
and asked the Rockets to “correct the error”.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:21.430
Daryl Morey took down his tweet and

00:06:21.430 --> 00:06:24.600
The Rockets tried to do damage control.

00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:28.520
“Yeah, We apologize, you know, we love China.

00:06:28.520 --> 00:06:30.640
We love playing there.”

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:35.240
Despite the apology, the government cancelled
NBA broadcasts on Chinese state TV.

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:40.420
“CCTV’s sports channel has just announced
that it will suspend the broadcast of any

00:06:40.420 --> 00:06:44.300
NBA games in China,
including this week’s preseason games.”

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:48.320
Tencent also suspended broadcasts
of Houston Rockets games.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:52.180
In Shanghai, workers were tearing down advertisements
for upcoming games

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:55.970
And fans protested outside the stadium where
teams were set to play.

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:02.900
On social media, a fan posted this video of
himself tearing up tickets to the game

00:07:02.900 --> 00:07:05.460
in support for the Chinese government.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:11.240
The NBA Commissioner stood behind
 Morey at a press conference.

00:07:11.240 --> 00:07:16.920
“The long held values of the NBA are to
support freedom of expression and, in this

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:22.320
case, Daryl Morey, as the general manager
of the Houston Rockets, enjoys that right.”

00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:24.620
But the league undercut their message when

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:27.940
journalists were stopped from asking
about the incident afterwards.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:32.680
“I just wonder after the events of this
week and the fallout we’ve seen, whether

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:37.660
you would both feel differently about speaking
out in that way in the future?”

00:07:42.660 --> 00:07:49.040
Foreign businesses in China, have long recognized
that there are red lines that must not be crossed.

00:07:49.040 --> 00:07:55.300
And traditionally those have been the three
Ts: Taiwan, Tibet, Tiananmen.

00:07:55.300 --> 00:07:59.940
Three political issues the Chinese government
tries to control messaging around.

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:05.080
Companies have learned that if you cross one
of these lines, there is a price to pay.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:11.460
For example, in 2018 Gap was selling this
t-shirt featuring a map of China without Taiwan.

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:16.060
After a photo of the shirt was posted online,
Gap apologized and promised to stop selling

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:21.500
the “incomplete” t-shirt in a statement
released by People’s Daily: a government newspaper.

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:29.820
You're talking about a one party state that has
 the ability to let you in, to expel you, to make you rich.

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:36.220
By withholding access to consumers,
China forces foreign companies to apologize

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:40.620
and/or change their message
 in order to continue selling products.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:46.220
Because China has a population of 1.4 billion,
that usually works.

00:08:46.230 --> 00:08:51.459
The NBA learned that doing business in China
means playing by their rules and that’s

00:08:51.459 --> 00:08:55.379
not what US leaders like President Clinton
had envisioned.

00:08:55.379 --> 00:09:00.839
The hope was that trade with China would open
it up to ideas of democracy and free speech,

00:09:00.839 --> 00:09:05.449
while making American companies 
money at the same time.

00:09:05.449 --> 00:09:09.500
But as American companies changed their messages
to sell products in China,

00:09:09.500 --> 00:09:14.100
the risk became importing Chinese censorship.

